  "query_preview": "/Sunday through Thursday/...\nA weak shortwave and accompanying cold front will track across \nthe Pacific Northwest on Sunday. Moisture and dynamics remain \nquite limited with this boundary, confining a slight chance of \nlight showers mainly to northern Baker County and the West \nCentral Mountains of Idaho. The remainder of southwest Idaho and\nsoutheast Oregon will stay dry with little more than passing \nmid and high clouds. \n\nBehind this system, the synoptic pattern amplifies substantially\nheading into early next week. A deep upper level trough\nestablishes itself off the Pacific Coast, pumping strong south-\nsouthwesterly flow and anomalous warmth northward into the Great\nBasin and northern Intermountain region. Temperatures will climb\nrapidly from Monday onward. By Wednesday, high temperatures are\nprojected to peak in the mid to upper 60s for lower elevation\nvalleys, which is roughly 15 degrees above seasonal normals.\nSeveral locations, including the Treasure Valley, could approach\nor exceed daily record highs. Dry and tranquil conditions will\npersist across the forecast area through mid-week under this\nstrong ridge before model guidance begins to hint at unsettled\nweather approaching late in the week as the offshore trough\nedges eastward.",
